mode: "100644"
text: "# The Deconstruction Framework\n\nA systematic teardown of a viral (or overperforming) piece — the layers to analyze, then how to\nisolate which layer actually drove it. The goal is the **mechanism**, not a description.\n\n## The layers — tear down each one\n\nGo through every layer and note what the piece did:\n\n1. **Hook (first 1–3 sec / first line)** — what stopped the scroll? The opening claim, image,\n   tension, or pattern interrupt. (See `hook-writer`.)\n2. **Emotional / share driver** — what did it make people *feel* (awe, anger, humor, inspiration,\n   recognition)? This is usually the real engine — see `why-things-spread.md`.\n3. **Retention structure** — how did it hold attention to the end (open loops, pacing, payoff,\n   loop)? High completion/rewatch is a core distribution signal.\n4. **Format / packaging** — the format (talking head, storytime, listicle, carousel), the title/\n   thumbnail/cover, on-screen text, length.\n5. **Topic / angle** — the specific take. Was it contrarian, novel, timely, deeply relatable?\n6. **Share-trigger** — *why did people send it to someone else?* (Identity, \"this is so us,\"\n   usefulness, social currency.) Virality = shares, so this layer is decisive.\n7. **Distribution factors** — the things *around* the content: the creator's existing audience size,\n   trending sound, posting time, a one-time news moment, platform boosts. Often miscredited as the\n   content's merit (see `replicability-and-application.md`).\n\nWrite a one-line note per layer. Resist the urge to praise everything — most layers are competent\nbut not the *cause*.\n\n## Isolate the real driver (the counterfactual test)\n\nA viral piece has many features; usually **only one or two actually drove it.** To find them, run\nthe counterfactual on each notable feature:\n\n> **\"Remove this — does it still pop?\"**\n\n- Remove the trending sound → would a strong, relatable hook + story still have spread? If yes, the\n  sound was incidental, not the driver.\n- Swap the hook for a flat one → does it die? If yes, the hook was load-bearing.\n- Strip the emotional turn → is it just a nice video? If yes, the *emotion* was the engine.\n\nWhatever the piece **can't lose without collapsing** is the driver. Whatever it could lose and still\nwork is noise. **Most bad analysis credits the noise** (the sound, the topic, the surface format) and\nmisses the load-bearing element (usually the hook + the emotional/share trigger).\n\n## Rate each layer: driver vs incidental\n\nFor each layer, tag it:\n\n- **DRIVER** — load-bearing; the piece fails without it. (Usually 1–2 layers.)\n- **SUPPORTING** — helped, but not the cause.\n- **INCIDENTAL** — present but didn't matter; don't copy it as if it did.\n- **CONFOUND** — a distribution factor (audience size, luck, one-time moment) that isn't a content\n  lesson at all → flag for the replicability check.\n\n## The output of a teardown\n\nA clean teardown produces: the **1–2 real drivers**, the **share-trigger**, the **confounds to\ndiscount**, and a one-sentence statement of the **mechanism** (\"a load-bearing relatable hook + an\nanger/justice emotional trigger drove the shares; the sound and topic were incidental\"). That\nmechanism is what you carry into `replicability-and-application.md`.\n"
